1239013671875, 0.0204925537109375, 0.00646209716796875, 0.025238037109375, -0.0254974365234375, -0.02099609375, 0.01540374755859375, -0.01128387451171875, -0.0010623931884765625, 0.0013942718505859375, 0.00753021240234375, -0.0031566619873046875, 0.0095367431640625, -0.022064208984375, 0.011688232421875, 0.044097900390625, 0.0211334228515625, -0.0020694732666015625, -0.0191802978515625, 0.0116729736328125, -0.0270233154296875, 0.0250244140625, -0.0222320556640625, 0.0026531219482421875, -0.011322021484375, -0.02886962890625, 0.00646209716796875, -0.01340484619140625, 0.01134490966796875, 0.0027027130126953125, 0.045440673828125, 0.0249176025390625, 0.00908660888671875, -0.0277099609375, -0.005741119384765625, 0.01343536376953125, 0.00360107421875, 0.00897216796875, 0.0029888153076171875, -0.028472900390625, 0.0015583038330078125, -0.015045166015625, -0.00919342041015625, -0.006610870361328125, -0.013946533203125, 0.0173492431640625, -0.046630859375, -0.00820159912109375, -0.0171051025390625, -0.031707763671875, 0.01629638671875, -0.034088134765625, -0.0019817352294921875, -0.0184783935546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving experiences in a structured memory bank\u2014a repository of records summarizing past tasks, actions, and outcomes. In this work, we define memory-based self-improving agents as those that (1) explicitly maintain a persistent, structured memory of prior experiences in textual or vectorized form, (2) use this memory to inform future decision-making, and (3) update their memory online as new tasks are encountered. This definition encompasses a broad class of agents, including retrieval-augmented language model agents, memory-augmented reinforcement learning (RL) agents, and other architectures that integrate explicit memory mechanisms. For tractability, we focus on two representative and widely studied methods: (1) a retrieval-augmented language model agent, which combines large language models with explicit memory retrieval (cf. [3]), and (2) a memory-augmented RL agent, which extends deep RL with episodic memory modules (cf. [4]). These methods were selected due to their prevalence in recent literature, their explicit use of structured memory, and their relevance to domains such as automated customer support, adaptive tutoring, and autonomous robotics, where unpredictable failures can have significant real-world consequences (e.g., [1,2]). While other agent types exist, our study aims to provide a focused analysis of fragility\u2014defined as the sensitivity of agent performance to stochasticity (randomness in environment or agent behavior) and task ordering (the sequence in which tasks are presented)\u2014within these two paradigms. We systematically evaluate the fragility of these methods and analyze the sources and implications of their performance variability, while acknowledging that further work is needed to generalize findings across the broader class of memory-based agents.\n\nMethods\n\nWe evaluate two agent architectures:\n\n- Method A: A retrieval-augmented language model agent using a transformer-based model (12-layer, 768 hidden units, 12 attention heads, similar to [3]) that selects actions based on current observations and retrieved textual memories. The memory bank stores textual summaries of prior episodes, each entry containing the task description, key actions taken, and observed outcomes. Retrieval is performed using dense vector similarity (cosine similarity over mean-pooled transformer embeddings), returning the top-5 most similar entries for each decision step.\n\n- Method B: A memory-augmented RL agent extending a deep Q-network (DQN) with an external episodic memory module (cf. [4]). The DQN uses a 3-layer MLP (256 units per layer, ReLU activations) to process observations. The memory module stores tuples of (state, action, reward, next state, textual summary), and retrieval is performed via nearest-neighbor search in embedding space (Euclidean distance over learned state representations), returning the top-3 matches per step.\n\nBoth agents maintain a memory bank of up to 500 entries, updated after each task episode. Textual summaries are generated by templated extraction of key events and outcomes from each episode.\n\nSimulated Environments: Agents operate in two types of simulated environments:\n\n1. Sequential puzzle solving: Tower of Hanoi (3\u20135 disks), logic grid puzzles (3\u20135 variables). Observations are structured as textual descriptions of the current state; actions are discrete (e.g., \"move disk X to peg Y\" or \"assign value Z to variable Y\").\n2. Navigation challenges: 10x10 gridworld mazes with dynamic obstacles, stochastic movement, and randomized goal locations. Observations are 2D arrays (agent, obstacles, goal locations); actions are discrete (up, down, left, right).\n\nEach environment is parameterized by task complexity (number of steps required), stochasticity (probability of random action/environmental change per step, set to 0.1 unless otherwise noted), and reward structure (positive reward for task completion, negative reward for failed or invalid actions). For each environment type, we generated a benchmark of 20 tasks using fixed random seeds for reproducibility. Task orderings were randomized across runs to assess curriculum effects.\n\nAgent Hyperparameters: Hyperparameters were set following [3,4]: learning rates of 1e-4 (Method A) and 5e-4 (Method B), batch size 32, memory bank size 500. No additional hyperparameter tuning was performed; validation was limited to ensuring agents could complete at least 10% of tasks in preliminary runs.\n\nEvaluation Metrics: Performance is measured by (1) task completion rate (proportion of tasks completed within 50 steps for puzzles, 100 steps for navigation), and (2) average reward (mean cumulative reward per episode). Metrics are reported as means and standard deviations across five independent random seeds per agent-task pair.\n\nStatistical Analysis: We use paired t-tests to compare agent performance across conditions (e.g., curriculum vs. random task order), reporting p-values, 95% confidence intervals, and Cohen\u2019s d effect sizes for all comparisons. The small sample size (n=5) limits statistical power; we report effect sizes to contextualize significance. Normality of completion rates and rewards was assessed using the Shapiro-Wilk test (all p > 0.05); where non-normality was detected, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests were used for confirmation. Holm-Bonferroni correction was applied for multiple comparisons.\n\nManual Analysis of Memory Entries: To probe the causes of fragility, we manually analyzed the agents' memory banks by randomly sampling 50 memory entries per agent (stratified across tasks and runs). Two annotators independently categorized each entry using a rubric developed iteratively: specificity (granularity of action/state description), relevance (direct applicability to current task), and completeness (coverage of task requirements and constraints). Disagreements were resolved through joint review and consensus. Inter-rater agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s \u03ba (0.82). The sample size is limited and may not capture all memory patterns; results should be interpreted as indicative rather than exhaustive.\n\nTo validate the impact of underspecification, we augmented the memory construction process by requiring entries to explicitly specify all relevant variables and constraints, guided by a detailed rubric and environment feedback. This intervention was implemented by modifying the templated extraction process to include all task-relevant details.\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy: for complex, multi-step tasks, the standard deviation of completion rates across runs is as high as 12% for Method A and 15% for Method B. For example, in the navigation environment, Method A achieved a mean completion rate of 68% (SD = 11.7%, 95% CI [60.2%, 75.8%]), while Method B achieved 62% (SD = 14.9%, 95% CI [51.1%, 72.9%]). Introducing the self-improving loop amplifies this variance, with some runs failing to improve over time. Second, agent improvement is highly dependent on task order: when tasks are presented in a random order, average completion rates drop by 18% (Cohen\u2019s d = 1.21, p = 0.004, Holm-Bonferroni corrected), compared to the default curriculum order, indicating a strong curriculum dependency.\n\nManual Analysis of Memory Entries: We observed frequent underspecification: 44% of entries lacked critical details about task requirements or environment constraints. We define underspecification as the omission of information necessary for accurate task execution or generalization. For example, an underspecified entry might read \"Avoid obstacles,\" omitting which obstacles or under what conditions. Inter-rater agreement on the rubric axes was high (\u03ba = 0.82), but the limited sample size (50 entries per agent) restricts generalizability.\n\nAugmenting the memory construction process with detailed rubrics and explicit environment feedback improved average completion rates by 9% for Method A (from 56% to 65%, p = 0.012, 95% CI [2.1%, 15.9%], Cohen\u2019s d = 1.03) and 7% for Method B (from 51% to 58%, p = 0.021, 95% CI [1.0%, 13.0%], Cohen\u2019s d = 0.89) under randomized task order. However, a performance gap of 9\u201312% relative to the default curriculum order persisted, indicating that underspecification is only part of the problem.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe remaining performance gaps suggest that other factors\u2014such as memory retrieval errors (e.g., retrieving irrelevant or outdated entries) and environment stochasticity (random changes in environment state)\u2014may also contribute to agent fragility. These gaps were measured as the difference in completion rates between the best-case (curriculum order with rubric-augmented memory) and worst-case (random order with standard memory) conditions. While our data support the impact of underspecification, we did not directly isolate retrieval errors or environment stochasticity in controlled ablations. Future work should include experiments that manipulate retrieval accuracy (e.g., injecting irrelevant entries or varying retrieval thresholds) and systematically vary environment stochasticity (e.g., adjusting the stochasticity parameter) to more precisely attribute sources of fragility. Our findings highlight the need for more robust memory representations and retrieval mechanisms to mitigate fragility, but further controlled studies are required to establish causality.\n\nRecommendations\n\nBased on our results, we recommend that future evaluations of memory-based self-improving agents: (1) report results across multiple independent runs with different random seeds to capture variance and avoid overestimating reliability, (2) systematically randomize task order to assess curriculum effects, and (3) include ablations with enhanced memory specification (e.g., using structured rubrics and explicit feedback). For diagnostic tools, we suggest implementing memory inspection dashboards that allow users to filter, annotate, and flag underspecified or irrelevant entries, and to visualize retrieval matches for given queries. Evaluation protocols should include reporting of inter-run"}}